  "worked_solution": "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2033}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x+16(\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x)=0.\\,\u2713\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x+16(\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x)=0.\\,\u2713\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2032} & {y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}-{y}_{1}^{\u2032}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x{(\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x)}^{\u2032}-{(\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x)}^{\u2032}\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x(\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x)\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}\u20614x+{sin}^{2}\u20614x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}\u20614x+B\\operatorname{sin}\u20614x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true.",
